Database migrations, managed by Flask-Migrate (Alembic).

Everyday use:

    flask db upgrade            # apply everything outstanding -- what Render runs
    flask db migrate -m "add cards.difficulty"   # write a migration from model changes
    flask db current            # which revision this database is on
    flask db history            # all revisions, newest first
    flask db downgrade -1       # step back one (check the downgrade() body first)

After changing a model, ALWAYS run `flask db migrate`, then read the generated file
before committing it. Autogenerate is good at columns and indexes and blind to a few
things -- a column rename looks like a drop plus an add, which silently discards the
data. Fix those by hand.

CI runs `flask db upgrade` followed by `flask db check` against a real Postgres. If the
models and the migrations disagree, the Migrations job fails, so a model change without
a migration cannot reach develop.

Do not edit a migration that has already been applied anywhere. Write a new one.
